 ### I'm on the Traversence team. What is staging?
-**System Configuration → Staging** is the copy of the site at staging.trvgo.us. It's the live site in safe mode: it acts like live, and nothing done there reaches the real world until it's published. The Founder sets it up once (**Set up staging**, which also gives the test accounts new sign-ins, shown once for the GitHub secret CHECK_ACCOUNTS together with staging's viewing password). It's no longer copied over every week: once **Keep staging caught up** is on (System Configuration → Staging), live's changes come into staging within a minute and what you do on staging is kept; **Start staging over** is a button for when a fresh copy is wanted, and says first what staging would lose. On staging, every email, text, payment and outside call is held, sandboxed or paced (see *What does staging hold, and where do I see it?*), email is readable on its own Staging page, payments use the sandboxes, paid AI and the crawler stay off unless their switch is on (**Switches → Staging: paid AI**, **Staging: the crawler**), and staff sign in there with the test staff logins. The trvgo.us router's copy answers at staging.trvgo.us/go/, so the word "staging" is kept: no GO Code or shop can take it. A fresh copy (Start staging over) goes a table at a time and carries on by itself on the server, so the page can be closed: its bar shows how far it's got, and whoever started it gets an email when it's done (or if it stops). **Carry on the refresh** nudges one that seems stuck. If **Open staging** ever meets a password prompt instead of letting you in, **Check the way in** (on the same page, Founder) asks staging for a page several ways (with nothing, with the pass, with the checks' sign-in, and the whole Open staging path) and says which part answered each: the web server, staging's guard or the page itself. It also shows the rules and settings on the way to staging's pages, never a password or key, with a **Copy the answers** button to send them to whoever is fixing it. Its first run found why Open staging kept asking: the rule that keeps staging's private folder shut also shut its pages, so its self-test put the browser's password prompt back. That's fixed, and Open staging lets you straight in.
